---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Hero from '~/components/widgets/Hero.astro';
import Content from '~/components/widgets/Content.astro';
import Features3 from '~/components/widgets/Features3.astro';
import CallToAction from '~/components/widgets/CallToAction.astro';
import titleSlide from '~/assets/images/css-animations-webinar-title.jpg';
import transformCode from '~/assets/images/css-animations-transform-code.png';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Modern Animations with SCSS/SASS Student Webinar — OpenClassrooms',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <Hero tagline="Guest Instructor · SCSS · Animations · Chrome DevTools">
    <Fragment slot="title">
      Modern Animations with SCSS —<br />
      <span class="text-accent dark:text-white">OpenClassrooms Student Webinar</span>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle">
      Invited to lead a live student webinar for OpenClassrooms covering modern CSS animations built with SCSS/SASS — including transitions, @keyframes, the 12 principles of animation applied to the web, and Chrome DevTools for debugging animation performance.
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <!-- Context -->
  <Content
    tagline="The Webinar"
    title="Animation principles built with SCSS"
    isReversed
  >
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full px-6 py-8">
        <Image src={titleSlide} alt="Modern CSS Animations Student Webinar — OpenClassrooms, December 10, 2020" class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700" />
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">On December 10, 2020, I delivered a live student webinar for OpenClassrooms as Part 2 of the CSS & SASS series — this time focused on building modern animations using SCSS/SASS, extending the course with live CodePen demos and a Chrome DevTools deep dive into animation performance.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">The session covered animation theory grounded in the 12 principles — using the Walt Disney/Mickey Mouse example to show how complex, believable motion is built from simple shapes and speed curves — then moved into practical CSS implementation with transitions, @keyframes, and the transform property.</p>
      <p>One practical rule I emphasized throughout: each CSS selector can only have one transform property. Because of CSS cascading, a second transform on the same selector will cancel out the first — a common source of bugs for students just learning animations.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-b"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- What I Covered -->
  <Features3
    tagline="The Content"
    title="What I covered"
    subtitle="Classic animation theory meets SCSS-powered implementation — grounded in live CodePen demos and real browser debugging."
    columns={3}
    items={[
      {
        title: '12 Principles of Animation',
        description: 'Introduced the classic Disney animation principles — squash and stretch, anticipation, staging, follow-through, and more — and showed how each maps to modern CSS and web animation practice.',
        icon: 'tabler:keyframes',
      },
      {
        title: 'How the Browser Renders CSS',
        description: 'Walked through the four browser rendering steps — style, layout, paint, and composite — explaining which CSS properties trigger expensive repaints and how to write animations that stay on the compositor.',
        icon: 'tabler:cpu',
      },
      {
        title: 'CSS Transitions',
        description: 'Covered the four ingredients of a CSS transition — the property to animate, its before and after values, duration, and a pseudo-selector trigger — with live CodePen examples showing each in action.',
        icon: 'tabler:arrows-exchange',
      },
      {
        title: '@keyframes Animations',
        description: 'Moved beyond simple A-to-B transitions into multi-stage @keyframes animations — building a progress bar example to show how to create multiple waypoints throughout an animation sequence.',
        icon: 'tabler:timeline',
      },
      {
        title: 'CSS Transforms',
        description: 'Demonstrated the transform property with perspective and rotateX — showing how 3D transforms work in CSS and how BEM modifiers can be used to create variants from a single base style.',
        icon: 'tabler:3d-rotate',
      },
      {
        title: 'Chrome DevTools for Animation',
        description: 'Used the Chrome DevTools animations panel to inspect, slow down, and profile animations — showing how to find jank, identify dropped frames, and use CPU throttling to test on slower devices.',
        icon: 'tabler:brand-chrome',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Live Demo -->
  <Content
    tagline="Live Demo"
    title="Transforms, perspective, and BEM modifiers"
  >
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full px-6 py-8">
        <Image src={transformCode} alt="CSS transform with perspective and BEM modifier variants — live demo code" class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-md border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700" />
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">One of the session's live demo highlights was combining CSS transforms with BEM-style modifier classes — using <code class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 px-1 rounded text-sm">perspective()</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 px-1 rounded text-sm">rotateX()</code> to create 3D effects and modifier variants like <code class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 px-1 rounded text-sm">&--perspective75px</code> to apply different depths.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">This reinforced the BEM naming convention from the previous webinar while introducing new CSS functionality — giving learners continuity across sessions and showing how the naming system scales into complex components.</p>
      <p>All examples were built live in CodePen so students could fork them, experiment, and keep the reference for later.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-e"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Resources -->
  <Content
    tagline="Resources Shared"
    title="Tools and libraries covered in the session"
  >
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">Throughout the webinar I shared a curated set of resources to help students go deeper on CSS animations — from quick-reference cheatsheets to full animation libraries used in professional work.</p>
      <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3">
        {[
          { name: 'CSS Animation Cheatsheet', description: 'A quick-reference summary of CSS animation properties and values' },
          { name: 'Animate.css', description: 'A popular ready-to-use animation library for common effects' },
          { name: 'Loaders.CSS', description: 'A collection of loading animations to deconstruct and learn from' },
          { name: 'Motion UI', description: "Foundation's animation library — useful if you prefer not to write animations from scratch" },
          { name: 'GreenSock (GSAP)', description: 'A professional JavaScript animation library — where CSS animation skills lead next' },
          { name: 'Catch a Fire', description: 'A nonprofit platform I recommended for building real portfolio work while helping organizations' },
        ].map(resource => (
          <div class="p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">{resource.name}</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted mt-1">{resource.description}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-b"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Video -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="text-center mb-8">
      <p class="text-base text-secondary dark:text-blue-200 font-bold tracking-wide uppercase mb-2">Webinar Recording</p>
      <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tighter font-heading">Watch the Session</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-muted mt-4">Full recording of the live Modern CSS Animations student webinar, December 10, 2020.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="relative w-full rounded-lg shadow-lg overflow-hidden" style="padding-top: 56.25%;">
      <iframe
        class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full"
        src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID_HERE"
        title="Modern CSS Animations Student Webinar — OpenClassrooms"
        frameborder="0"
        allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
        allowfullscreen
      ></iframe>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Reflection -->
  <Content
    tagline="Reflection"
    title="Teaching animation as a performance discipline"
    isReversed
  >
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full py-12">
        <div class="text-center">
          <p class="text-8xl font-bold font-heading text-accent leading-none">2020</p>
          <p class="text-xl text-muted mt-3">OpenClassrooms</p>
          <div class="mt-8 space-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
            <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Live Student Webinar</p>
            <p>CSS Animations · @keyframes · DevTools</p>
            <p>Global Audience — US & Europe</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">What makes SCSS particularly powerful for animation is the same thing that makes it powerful for any stylesheet — structure. Nesting keeps animation code tied to its component, variables make easing and duration consistent, and BEM modifiers let you create animation variants without duplicating @keyframes blocks.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">This session was also a chance to show learners how the Chrome DevTools animation panel reveals what's actually happening under the hood — making it possible to tune timing, catch jank, and test performance across slower devices.</p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {['SCSS', 'SASS', 'CSS Animations', '@keyframes', 'CSS Transitions', 'Chrome DevTools', 'OpenClassrooms'].map(tag => (
          <span class="bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-900 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-200 text-sm font-medium px-3 py-1 rounded-full">{tag}</span>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-4">Live student webinar on modern animations with SCSS/SASS, delivered for OpenClassrooms, December 10, 2020.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <CallToAction
    actions={[
      { variant: 'primary', text: 'View All Projects', href: '/projects' },
      { text: 'Get in Touch', href: '/about#contact' },
    ]}
  >
    <Fragment slot="title">Interested in working together?</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle">I'm open to remote opportunities in instructional design and technical learning experience design.</Fragment>
  </CallToAction>
</Layout>
